// maxon-shv2 compiler entry point (M1 driver).
//
// Parses the command line, dispatches a command, and maps the driver's
// CompileError outcomes to an exit code. `build` reads the input file/directory,
// runs the pipeline, and writes an executable. `verify-warm-rebuild` is the M2
// warm-rebuild gate: it asserts the query spine is deterministic + incremental for
// a file and exits 0 iff both properties hold. Diagnostics (E3001/E3002) are
// printed by `compile`/`compileToCodeResult`; Main stays silent on the
// `compileError` arm so a failing build's stderr is EXACTLY the reported
// diagnostics (the `maxoncstderr` spec contract).
//
// CLI (documented):
//   maxon-shv2 build <file|directory>... [-o <output>] [--emit-ir] [--emit-ir-runtime=<name>,…] [--target=<cpu>-<os>]
//   maxon-shv2 verify-warm-rebuild <file>
//   maxon-shv2 spec-test [dir] [--filter=<pattern>] [--update-required] [--network] [--workers=<n>] [--target=<cpu>-<os>]

// The default spec directory `spec-test` scans when no directory is given
// (resolved against the process cwd — the runner is invoked from the repo root).
let DefaultSpecDir = "specs-shv2"

// ⭐ **THE EXIT CODE THAT MEANS "NOTHING RAN".** Distinct from 1 (a command that RAN and reported
// failures) and from 101 (a leak) on purpose: "nothing ran" and "something failed" are different
// facts, and a caller reading only the exit code — a shell script, CI, the rung gate battery — must be
// able to tell them apart. Same number and same meaning as `cross-target-gate.sh`'s own refusal.
//
// TWO refusals return it, and they belong to one family: a binary older than the sources it was built
// from (`refuseIfStale`) and a checkout another command is already writing (`acquireTreeLock`). Both
// say the same thing to the reader — the tree is not in a state to be measured, fix that and re-run —
// so the number is declared once, HERE, where the driver's exit codes are decided.
module let NothingRanExitCode = 2

// The CLI spellings the spec-test WORKER POOL has to re-emit, because a worker is this same
// executable re-launched with this same command line (see SpecWorkerPool). Named here — where the
// parser that reads them back also lives — so the two halves of that command line cannot drift
// apart into a hang. The rest of the driver's flags are matched inline: they are read here and
// nowhere else.
module let SpecTestCommandName = "spec-test"

module let WorkerPersistentFlag = "--worker-persistent"

module let FilterFlagPrefix = "--filter="

module let UpdateRequiredFlag = "--update-required"

// ⭐ `--network` — RUN THE CASES THAT REACH A REAL EXTERNAL HOST. The cases marked `<!-- network: live -->`
// across `tcp-client`, `async-tcp` and `http-client` open a socket to `tcpbin.com` / `httpbin.org`; a default
// run leaves them out, because a suite whose verdict turns on somebody else's uptime is not a claim about this
// compiler. The cases stay ACTIVE and PARSED — the marker sits on the CASE, never shelved as
// `disabled-test:` — and this flag selects them (see `SpecTestRunner.networkModeAllows`), which is why
// EXCLUDING them is announced rather than silent (`reportNetworkExclusions`).
//
// ⛔ **NO NUMBER IS WRITTEN HERE, DELIBERATELY.** "Seven" stood in six places in this harness while the
// corpus held EIGHT — transcribed from v1's hard-coded roster by the very comment arguing such a roster rots
// (`SpecParser.NetworkMarker`). The marker is the census; a count beside it is a second one.
//
// It is in the re-emitted set because the parent forwards it to every worker: both halves of the pool run
// the same selection rule, and a worker that never heard the flag would answer for tests nobody counted.
module let NetworkFlag = "--network"

// ⛔⛔ **`--compiler=<path>` IS WITHDRAWN, AND THE SPELLING IS KEPT SO THE REFUSAL CAN NAME IT.**
//
// It named a DIFFERENT BINARY to build each selected test with, which was a real and separate fact
// from "the executable a worker process is" for exactly as long as a test was built by a SUBPROCESS.
// `spec-test` now compiles every case IN THIS PROCESS (G20), so the compiler under test is this
// binary's own code and there is nothing a path could select. ⚖ **USER RULING 2026-08-22: in-process
// only, no dual path** — a flag kept as a no-op would be a command line that reads as though it chose
// something.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS REFUSED BY NAME, NOT LEFT TO THE ANONYMOUS `badOption` PATH.** A script still passing it
// was RIGHT to, until this rung; "unknown option: --compiler=…" would tell its author that the driver
// never heard of it, which is false and sends them looking for a typo. See `withdrawnOptionMessage`.
let WithdrawnCompilerFlagPrefix = "--compiler"

// `--target=` is in this re-emitted set (not with `build`'s options) because a cross-target
// spec run forwards it to every worker's command line — see SpecWorkerPool.spawnNoWait. The
// parser below reads it back through this same constant, so the two halves cannot drift.
module let TargetFlagPrefix = "--target="

let WorkersFlagPrefix = "--workers="

// ⭐ **THE TWO IR-SIDECAR SPELLINGS.** ⛔ They used to be EXPORTED *"because the spec runner emits
// them on the command line it spawns each fragment build with"* — and it does not: since G20 the spec
// runner passes `emitIr` and `runtimeIrNames` as ARGUMENTS to `Compiler.compileToProduct`, so these
// two are read here and nowhere else, exactly like the rest of `build`'s options.
//
// `--emit-ir` writes the lowered Target IR beside the exe. `--emit-ir-runtime=<name>,<name>` says
// which WITHHELD functions that IR must ALSO render: a golden fragment otherwise shows only the
// functions parsed from the PROGRAM's own source, so `mrt_start`, the allocator and the
// `__str_*`/`__managed_*` families carry no golden at all — and neither does a `stdlib/` body such as
// `String.trim`, which shv2 compiles into the same module and this withholds for the same reason (see
// `TargetPrinter.printTargetModule`). Naming them is per-BUILD and
// opt-in, so every fragment that does not ask is byte-for-byte what it was before the flag existed.
let EmitIrFlag = "--emit-ir"

// `--async-trace` — emit the green-thread trace (`spawn #`, `io_yield #`, `io_resume #`, `await #`) to stderr.
// The spec harness asks for the same thing per case, for an `<!-- AsyncTrace -->` fragment, but it asks
// `Compiler.compileToProduct` directly (G20) rather than by spelling this flag. Opt-in and per-BUILD, so a
// program that does not ask is byte-for-byte what it was before the flag existed — the trace CALLS are not
// emitted at all rather than being branched over at run time (see `GtRuntime.GtTraceEmitName`).
let AsyncTraceFlag = "--async-trace"

// ⭐ `--debugstream` — emit the DebugStream PRODUCER: the shared-memory ring `maxon monitor` decodes,
// plus the mm_alloc / mm_incref / mm_decref / mm_free events the memory manager writes into it. The spec
// harness asks for it per case, for an mm-trace fragment, through `Compiler.compileToProduct` — exactly as
// it asks for `--async-trace`'s producer.
//
// ⚠⚠ **OPT-IN AND PER-BUILD, AND THAT IS A CORRECTNESS PROPERTY RATHER THAN A COURTESY.** With the flag
// off NOT ONE instruction of this changes: the box header does not grow, `__mm_alloc`'s arity does not
// change, no event site is branched over. Thousands of committed `.test` fragment goldens depend on it.
// See `DebugStreamRuntime`'s header for the two-level gating rule this is the compile-time half of.
let DebugStreamFlag = "--debugstream"
let EmitIrRuntimeFlagPrefix = "--emit-ir-runtime="

// `--metrics=<path>`, exported because the SCALE runner spawns one build per rung with it
// (`ScaleTestRunner.compileRung`). ⛔ The spec runner used to spawn one per test as well, and read the
// `total` row back for its cumulative-CPU indicator; it now takes that number straight off
// `CompileProduct.totalCpuTicks` (G20), which is the same number from the same whole-compile probe
// without the file. It was written out as a literal at the parser below AND at the scale runner
// until a second harness would have made a third copy.
module let MetricsFlagPrefix = "--metrics="

// Comma, matching every other list-valued flag and directive this project reads (`--target`s in a
// `<!-- targets: … -->` marker, the reference's own `TargetsDirectiveRegex`). One separator, so a
// reader never has to ask which list is spelled which way.
let RuntimeNameSeparator = ","

function main() returns ExitCode
	let args = MaxonArgs.parse()

	// ⭐ A WITHDRAWN OPTION IS REPORTED BEFORE AN UNKNOWN ONE, and the order is the point: this driver
	// once accepted it, so its author needs to be told WHAT REPLACED IT rather than sent looking for a
	// typo. `firstBadOption`'s "the first one typed wins" rule decides between two equally anonymous
	// flags and does not reach across that difference.
	if not args.withdrawnOption.isEmpty() 'withdrawnOption'
		printError("{withdrawnOptionMessage(args.withdrawnOption)}\n")
		printUsage()
		return 1
	end 'withdrawnOption'

	// An option the driver does not implement aborts BEFORE any command runs. Every
	// command below would otherwise proceed as though the flag had been honoured,
	// which is the one outcome a caller can neither see nor recover from.
	if not args.badOption.isEmpty() 'badOption'
		printError("error: unknown option: {args.badOption}\n")
		printUsage()
		return 1
	end 'badOption'

	return match args.command 'cmd'
		build gives runBuild(args)
		verifyWarmRebuild gives runVerifyWarmRebuild(args)
		verifyRecheck gives runVerifyRecheck(args)
		lspSelfTest gives runLspSelfTest()
		specTest gives runSpecTest(args)
		scaleTest gives runScaleTest(args)
		unknown gives unknownCommand()
	end 'cmd'
end 'main'

// The outcome of resolving a `--target=<cpu>-<os>` spelling: either the Target it
// names, or the raw spelling for the error message when it names no known target.
module union TargetResolution
	resolved(target Target)
	unrecognized(spec String)
end 'TargetResolution'

// Resolve a `--target` spelling to a Target, or report it unrecognized. The targets
// maxon-shv2 can build for are listed once, and matched by their own `targetToString`
// spelling — so the parser and the printer read the same table and cannot drift into
// accepting a spelling the compiler cannot name back. A spec that names none of them
// is rejected; the list grows as backends land.
//
// EXPORTED because `GoldenTracking.reportUncomparedGoldens` asks the identical question of a
// `fragments/<name>` LANE DIRECTORY's name — a lane is named by a target spelling, so "which target is
// this?" has one answer and one reader. A private copy of the loop there would be a second table in all
// but name, and its failure mode is a census that quietly stops covering the next backend that lands.
module function parseBuildTarget(spec String) returns TargetResolution
	for candidate in supportedTargets() 'each'
		if targetToString(candidate) == spec 'match'
			return TargetResolution.resolved(candidate)
		end 'match'
	end 'each'
	return TargetResolution.unrecognized(spec)
end 'parseBuildTarget'

// ⭐ **EVERY TARGET THIS COMPILER CAN BUILD FOR — the one list.**
//
// It used to sit inline in the loop above, which was fine while the flag parser was the only thing that
// wanted it. `SpecVerdictSelfTest` wants it too — its whole subject is which lane this host routes each
// target to, and it must sweep ALL of them or it is a check scoped to whichever target its author
// happened to think of. A second array spelled there would agree today and would then quietly stop
// covering the next backend that lands, which is the one it would exist to catch.
module function supportedTargets() returns TargetArray
	return [Target.create(CpuArch.x64, os: Os.windows), Target.create(CpuArch.x64, os: Os.linux), Target.create(CpuArch.arm64, os: Os.macos), Target.create(CpuArch.arm64, os: Os.linux), Target.create(CpuArch.wasm32, os: Os.wasi)]
end 'supportedTargets'

// Resolve the optional `--target` flag shared by `build` and `spec-test`: the host target
// (`detectHostTarget`) when the flag is empty, otherwise whatever `parseBuildTarget` makes of the
// spelling (`unrecognized` when it names no known target). Folding the host default into one place
// keeps the two commands from drifting on what "no --target" means.
function resolveTargetFlag(spec String) returns TargetResolution
	if spec.isEmpty() 'host'
		return TargetResolution.resolved(detectHostTarget())
	end 'host'
	return parseBuildTarget(spec)
end 'resolveTargetFlag'

// Report a `--target` spelling that names no known target, as an exit code the
// build command returns directly. Separate from the match arm because a match arm
// is one statement, and this one both prints and yields the code.
function reportUnknownTarget(spec String) returns ExitCode
	printError("error: unknown --target '{spec}' (expected e.g. x64-windows, x64-linux or arm64-macos)\n")
	return 1
end 'reportUnknownTarget'

// ⭐⭐ **`build` TAKES A LIST, AND THE LIST'S ORDER IS PART OF WHAT IT SAYS (A3m).** Every positional is
// compiled, in the order given; `Compiler.loadSourceFiles` registers them in exactly that order and
// roots the project at their common ancestor. Before A3m the parser kept the FIRST positional and
// dropped the rest in silence — MEASURED, `build alias.maxon main.maxon` reported `E3001: No 'main'
// function found` and `build main.maxon alias.maxon` reported `E3011: Unknown type 'Int'`, each a
// diagnostic about a program the caller never asked for.
function runBuild(args MaxonArgs) returns ExitCode
	if args.paths.count() == 0 'noPath'
		printError("Usage: maxon-shv2 build <file|directory>... [-o <output>] [--emit-ir] [--emit-ir-runtime=<name>,...] [--target=<cpu>-<os>]\n")
		return 1
	end 'noPath'

	var inputPaths = MaxonSourcePathArray.create()
	for spelling in args.paths 'eachPositional'
		let path = try FilePath.from(spelling) otherwise 'badPath'
			printError("error: invalid path: {spelling}\n")
			return 1
		end 'badPath'
		inputPaths.push(path)
	end 'eachPositional'

	var target = detectHostTarget()
	match resolveTargetFlag(args.targetSpec) 'targetSel'
		resolved(t) then target = t
		unrecognized(spec) then return reportUnknownTarget(spec)
	end 'targetSel'

	if args.debugStream and not debugStreamSupports(target) 'unsupportedDebugStream'
		printError("error: {DebugStreamFlag} is not implemented for {targetToString(target)}. The DebugStream producer opens a NAMED shared segment (OpenFileMappingA / MapViewOfFile), reads an activation environment variable and stamps a millisecond stopwatch (GetTickCount64) — three host primitives shv2 has only on x64-windows, whose Std ops (`osOpenNamedSegment`, `osMapNamedSegment`, `osTickCountMs`) refuse every other lowering. Build for x64-windows, or implement those three for this target.\n")
		return 1
	end 'unsupportedDebugStream'

	let lockExit = acquireBuildTreeLock(inputPaths, outputOverride: args.outputOverride)
	if lockExit != 0 'busyTree'
		return lockExit
	end 'busyTree'

	let buildExit = compileAndReport(inputPaths, target: target, args: args)
	releaseTreeLock()
	return buildExit
end 'runBuild'

// ⭐ **WHICH BUILDS ARE TREE-LEVEL, and therefore take this checkout's lock: a PROJECT build that was
// not told where to put its output.** That is `maxon build maxon-shv2` — the invocation that writes
// `<project>/.maxon/` and the one that actually raced (see `TreeLock`).
//
// ⚠ **AND IT IS WHAT KEEPS THE SUITE OFF THE LOCK.** The spec runner compiles every selected test
// through a real `build` subprocess and the scale runner one per rung — thousands of them, in
// parallel, INSIDE a run that already holds the lock. Every one of those passes `-o`, because the
// harness owns the output path; so `-o` is exactly the line between "put it where the tree keeps its
// products" and "put it where I said", and locking only the former is what makes a nested build a
// non-event rather than a deadlock.
//
// A FILE build takes nothing either, for the same reason from the other end: it writes beside the file
// it was handed, which is a scratch directory whenever it matters.
//
// ⛔⛔ **A `count() != 1` BYPASS SAT HERE AND IT WAS A3m'S OWN REGRESSION** (BATCH10 review). Before
// A3m `build` took ONE path and silently dropped the rest, so a multi-path build did not exist and
// `count() > 1` was unreachable; A3m made it real and the guard was not widened with it. The bypass
// said a build naming several paths "writes nothing this lock protects, because its output name comes
// from the first SOURCE FILE rather than from `<project>/.maxon/`" — and `resolveOutputPath` derives a
// DIRECTORY build's name from the first registered source file too, by the same line of the same
// function. A fact both shapes share cannot be what tells them apart. `build maxon-shv2/Compiler
// maxon-shv2/Testing` wrote into the checkout with no lock at all.
//
// ⇒ **THE RULE IS THE ONE `TreeLock`'s HEADER ALREADY STATES, ASKED OF THE PROJECT ROOT INSTEAD OF OF
// THE ARGUMENT LIST**: the lock covers the checkout *"containing the path the command writes into"*,
// and *"a `-o` build writes where the CALLER said and takes nothing"*. So there are exactly two
// questions, and neither is how many paths were typed:
//
//   · **Did the caller say where the output goes?** That is `-o`, and it is now asked FIRST and of
//     every shape. It is the whole reason the suite's thousands of nested `build … -o …` compiles are
//     a non-event inside a run that already holds the lock.
//   · **Otherwise the output is DERIVED — into WHAT?** `resolveOutputPath` writes beside the input
//     when the root is an existing FILE, and beside the first registered source otherwise. So the
//     question is whether the project ROOT is a directory, i.e. whether this build lands in a TREE or
//     beside one named file.
//
// ⚠ **AND THE ROOT IS ASKED OF `commonAncestorOf`, THE SAME FUNCTION `loadProject` ROOTS THE PROJECT
// AT.** Not a second derivation: the lock and the compile cannot disagree about which tree is being
// written because there is one answer to that. It also makes the single-path case provably unmoved —
// `commonAncestorOf` returns its argument untouched when nothing narrows, so `build <file>` and
// `build <directory>` reach the identical test they always did.
//
// A root with no `stdlib/` above it takes no lock regardless: `acquireTreeLock` answers that itself
// (`maxon-shv2 build /tmp/scratch.maxon` has nothing shared to guard), which is why there is no
// separate scratch-path arm here.
// ⛔ **`--debugstream` IS REFUSED ON A TARGET IT IS NOT BUILT FOR, RATHER THAN SILENTLY EMITTING
// NOTHING.** The whole failure mode of an instrument is an EMPTY answer that reads as "the program did
// none of that" — the reference records exactly that costing it a day when the monitor died before
// spawning the child — so a target with no producer must say so at the command line, loudly, before
// anything is compiled. It is refused HERE, beside the other flag refusals, rather than as a diagnostic:
// there is no source span to hang it on, because nothing in the PROGRAM asked for this. The three Std
// ops' own lowerings panic on any other target, but that is a backstop for a compiler bug that got past
// this gate, never the user-facing rejection.
//
// ⚠ **IT GUARDS THE COMMAND LINE AND ONLY THE COMMAND LINE.** `runBuild` asks it; `compileToProduct`
// does not, and since G20 the spec harness calls that directly with a per-case `debugStream`. The
// harness carries its own gate for the same fact (`SpecTestRunner.targetCanCaptureMmTrace`, which
// SKIPS such a case on any other target rather than compiling it), so nothing reaches the lowerings
// by that road either — but the two are now separate doors onto one rule, and this one is no longer
// the outer of the pair.
function debugStreamSupports(target Target) returns bool
	return target.cpu == CpuArch.x64 and target.os == Os.windows
end 'debugStreamSupports'

function acquireBuildTreeLock(inputPaths MaxonSourcePathArray, outputOverride String) returns ExitCode
	if not outputOverride.isEmpty() 'callerOwnsTheOutput'
		return 0
	end 'callerOwnsTheOutput'

	// A list with no common root is refused by the compile moments later, with a message naming both
	// ends (`CompileError.noCommonRoot`). Taking no lock is right for it: there is no one tree it
	// writes into, which is the very thing that makes it unbuildable.
	let root = try commonAncestorOf(inputPaths) otherwise 'noTreeToLock'
		return 0
	end 'noTreeToLock'

	if not Directory.isDirectory(root) 'writesBesideOneNamedFile'
		return 0
	end 'writesBesideOneNamedFile'

	return acquireTreeLock(root)
end 'acquireBuildTreeLock'

// The build itself, split out so `runBuild` has ONE exit through which the tree lock is released.
// Maxon has no `defer`, and a release written at each of two returns is a release the third one
// forgets — the lock would then survive its holder and be broken 60 s later as abandoned, which is
// correct behaviour covering for a bug.
function compileAndReport(inputPaths MaxonSourcePathArray, target Target, args MaxonArgs) returns ExitCode
	let outputPath = try compile(inputPaths, target: target, outputOverride: args.outputOverride, emitIr: args.emitIr, metricsPath: args.metricsPath, runtimeIrNames: args.runtimeIrNames, asyncTrace: args.asyncTrace, debugStream: args.debugStream) otherwise (e) 'compileErr'
		reportCompileError(e)
		return 1
	end 'compileErr'

	print("Compiled -> {outputPath}\n")
	return 0
end 'compileAndReport'

// The M2 warm-rebuild gate: assert the query spine is deterministic and
// incremental for `<file>`. Exit 0 iff both properties hold. On a file/IO error
// the specific message is printed; a `compileError` stays silent (its diagnostics
// were already printed by the pipeline).
function runVerifyWarmRebuild(args MaxonArgs) returns ExitCode
	let usage = "maxon-shv2 verify-warm-rebuild <file>"
	if refusedPositionalBeyond(args, allowed: 1, usage: usage) 'tooManyPaths'
		return 1
	end 'tooManyPaths'

	let spelling = args.solePositional()
	if spelling.isEmpty() 'noPath'
		printError("Usage: {usage}\n")
		return 1
	end 'noPath'

	let inputPath = try FilePath.from(spelling) otherwise 'badPath'
		printError("error: invalid path: {spelling}\n")
		return 1
	end 'badPath'

	let target = detectHostTarget()
	let bothPass = try verifyWarmRebuild(inputPath, target: target) otherwise (e) 'verifyErr'
		reportCompileError(e)
		return 1
	end 'verifyErr'

	if bothPass 'passed'
		return 0
	end 'passed'

	return 1
end 'runVerifyWarmRebuild'

// The `verify-recheck <file|dir>` command: assert one `Project` survives being checked more than
// once. Shaped exactly like `runVerifyWarmRebuild` next door — same single-positional contract, same
// host target, and the same "the gate printed its own reasons" exit rule.
function runVerifyRecheck(args MaxonArgs) returns ExitCode
	let usage = "maxon-shv2 verify-recheck <file|dir>"
	if refusedPositionalBeyond(args, allowed: 1, usage: usage) 'tooManyPaths'
		return 1
	end 'tooManyPaths'

	let spelling = args.solePositional()
	if spelling.isEmpty() 'noPath'
		printError("Usage: {usage}\n")
		return 1
	end 'noPath'

	let inputPath = try FilePath.from(spelling) otherwise 'badPath'
		printError("error: invalid path: {spelling}\n")
		return 1
	end 'badPath'

	let target = detectHostTarget()
	let allPass = try verifyRecheck(inputPath, target: target) otherwise (e) 'verifyErr'
		reportCompileError(e)
		return 1
	end 'verifyErr'

	if allPass 'passed'
		return 0
	end 'passed'

	return 1
end 'runVerifyRecheck'

// The `lsp-selftest` command: run the LSP-internal assertion tables. Compiler-internal code that no
// `specs-shv2` case can reach — a spec case is a program shv2 compiles, and it can call `stdlib/` and
// nothing else — so it is gated the way the bootstrap gates its own such code, with a selftest.
function runLspSelfTest() returns ExitCode
	if runLspPositionSelfTest() 'passed'
		return 0
	end 'passed'

	return 1
end 'runLspSelfTest'

// The `spec-test [dir]` command: run every active test in `dir` (default `specs-shv2`) on a pool of
// persistent worker subprocesses, print per-test PASS/FAIL + an `N passed, M failed` summary, and
// exit non-zero iff any test failed.
//
// ⛔⛔ **ONE EXECUTABLE AGAIN — AND THIS HEADER SPENT A PARAGRAPH ARGUING THERE WERE TWO.** It read:
// *"TWO EXECUTABLES, NOT ONE … `workerExe` what a pool worker IS … `compilerExe` the COMPILER UNDER
// TEST — the binary each selected test is built with. This process unless `--compiler=<path>` names
// another."* Both facts were real, and the second existed only because a test was BUILT BY A
// SUBPROCESS whose executable a caller could name. Since G20 every test is compiled IN THIS PROCESS,
// so "the compiler under test" is this binary's own code: there is nothing left for a path to select,
// and `--compiler=` is WITHDRAWN rather than kept as a flag that could only lie (⚖ user ruling
// 2026-08-22 — in-process only, no dual path). `harnessExe` is `Process.executablePath()`, and it is
// both facts: what a worker is relaunched as, and what compiles every case. Not settable — a flag that
// spawned some OTHER harness as a worker never had a caller either.
//
// ⚠ **WHAT THE SPLIT WAS FOR IS NOT LOST.** The 🚩 Phase 1 gate was precisely the configuration that
// separated them — a harness compiled by maxon.exe driving maxon-shv2.exe as the compiler — and it is
// CLOSED (2026-08-22: the two harnesses agreed byte for byte). Its maintenance form is a self-build
// fixpoint diff, which is a question one binary can answer.
//
// There is ONE path through the pool, taken by every worker count including `--workers=1`. A serial
// in-process branch for the single-worker case would be a second implementation of "run the suite",
// and the two would drift — which is exactly what the worker-count-invariance gate exists to catch.
function runSpecTest(args MaxonArgs) returns ExitCode
	if refusedPositionalBeyond(args, allowed: 1, usage: "maxon-shv2 spec-test [dir] [--filter=<pattern>] [--update-required] [--network] [--workers=<n>] [--target=<cpu>-<os>]") 'tooManyPaths'
		return 1
	end 'tooManyPaths'

	let specDir = try resolveSpecDir(args) otherwise 'badSpecDir'
		printError("error: invalid spec directory: {args.solePositional()}\n")
		return 1
	end 'badSpecDir'

	// This binary: what a worker process is relaunched as, and what compiles every selected test. See
	// this function's header for why that is one value again.
	let harnessExe = try Process.executablePath() otherwise 'noExe'
		printError("error: could not determine the running executable's path\n")
		return 1
	end 'noExe'

	// The effective target for this run: the host unless `--target` overrode it. Resolved up front so
	// BOTH sides run the SAME target — the dispatcher's pool AND a relaunched worker, which re-parses
	// the same `--target` from its own command line (SpecWorkerPool.spawnNoWait forwards it) and
	// reaches this same resolution. A bad spelling fails once, here. The target reaches each selected
	// test's build (`--target=<cpu>-<os>`) and, for a cross target, its run under the vendored runtime.
	// Resolving before the worker branch prints nothing on the success path (only an unrecognized
	// spelling prints, to stderr, and returns), so the worker's stdout wire stays untouched.
	var effectiveTarget = detectHostTarget()
	match resolveTargetFlag(args.targetSpec) 'targetSel'
		resolved(t) then effectiveTarget = t
		unrecognized(spec) then return reportUnknownTarget(spec)
	end 'targetSel'

	// A WORKER, not a dispatcher: this process was spawned by a parent's pool and speaks the job
	// protocol on stdin/stdout instead of running a suite and reporting on it. It must be checked
	// before anything is printed — a worker's stdout is a wire, not a report.
	if args.workerPersistent 'isPoolWorker'
		// ⚠ A WORKER ASKS NOTHING BEFORE IT STARTS, and that is deliberate rather than an omission. It
		// used to ask `requireRunnerAvailable` — because a worker is a second door onto the GOLDEN WRITER
		// and a `maxoncstderr` case reaches that writer without ever launching a binary — and the point
		// of asking was to PANIC for a target this host cannot execute. That refusal is gone (it threw
		// away a whole lane's worth of checkable work), so the mint door is guarded where the writing
		// happens instead: see `SpecTestRunner.writeFragmentFile`'s `mayMint`.
		return runPersistentWorker(specDir, filter: args.filter, updateRequired: args.updateRequired, effectiveTarget: effectiveTarget, includeNetwork: args.includeNetwork)
	end 'isPoolWorker'

	// ⭐⭐ **GET OUT OF THE WAY BEFORE SATURATING THE MACHINE.** A full suite runs a pool of compiling
	// subprocesses and will use every core it is given; at NORMAL priority that makes the box unusable
	// while it runs. Below-normal costs nothing on an idle machine — MEASURED at 82.8 s against 83.1 s on
	// the bootstrap's suite at a fixed worker count, inside the noise — because a de-prioritised process
	// still gets every idle core. What it buys is that a foreground process preempts the pool instantly.
	//
	// ⚠ **AFTER THE WORKER BRANCH, AND THAT IS THE POINT: A WORKER MUST NOT CALL THIS.** Children inherit
	// the priority class, so the one call here reaches every worker this dispatcher spawns AND every test
	// binary those workers launch. Calling it again in a worker would be the same fact applied twice.
	//
	// ⚠ The answer is DISCARDED here and that is not a swallowed failure: on this lane the only documented
	// way to fail is an invalid handle, and the handle is a compile-time constant. What the value is FOR is
	// `specs-shv2/process-background-priority.md`, which asserts it to tell a working lowering from one that
	// did nothing — a question a caller cannot ask about itself.
	__Builtins.enterBackgroundPriority()

	// Whether the binaries this run is ABOUT to draw conclusions from are older than the tree they
	// were built from, asked before anything is selected. A stale binary reports a verdict about code
	// that is not here — a false red, or the same lie as a false green — so the honest answer is to
	// run nothing at all. Asked by the PARENT for `requireRunnerAvailable`'s reason, and asked AFTER
	// the worker branch above so a worker never re-asks it: one fact, reported once, on the process
	// whose stdout is a report rather than a wire.
	// The role reads as the SUBJECT of "<role> is STALE" (see `refuseIfStale`), so it is a bare noun
	// phrase: a trailing relative clause put the verb behind "…it tests is STALE" and the sentence
	// stopped parsing as English.
	let freshnessExit = refuseIfStale(harnessExe, role: "the spec-test harness (which is also the compiler it tests)")
	if freshnessExit != 0 'staleBinary'
		return freshnessExit
	end 'staleBinary'

	// Whether the RUNNER this target needs is installed, asked once and by the PARENT. A cross-target
	// run whose runner is missing fails identically for every test, so discovering it per test turns
	// one fact into hundreds of failures — and inside a worker, into a dead pipe whose real message is
	// in another process's stderr. The workers inherit the answer by never starting.
	//
	// ⚠ A target with NO runner on this host at all is not refused here — it is a lane that compiles
	// and compares everything and executes nothing, reported per case. See `requireRunnerAvailable`.
	requireRunnerAvailable(effectiveTarget)

	// ⭐ **AND THAT A VERDICT NOTHING RAN IS STILL COUNTED, REASONED AND NOT GREEN** — the mapping that
	// lane rests on, asked in the same slot and for `requireRunnerAvailable`'s reason. It is pure and
	// costs microseconds; see `SpecVerdictSelfTest` for why a lane whose whole job is reporting
	// "not run" needs a guard that the reporting cannot quietly become "passed".
	requireSpecVerdictMapping()

	// The hand-built ladders' index against the ladders themselves, asked once and by the PARENT for
	// `requireRunnerAvailable`'s reason. It rides the suite because the suite is the thing that already
	// runs: a check nobody runs is exactly the drift it exists to catch, one level up (see LadderIndex).
	requireLadderIndexComplete()

	// ⭐ **AND THAT THE BOOTSTRAP'S HALF OF THE TREE LOCK STILL SPEAKS THIS ONE'S FORMAT**, asked
	// immediately BEFORE the lock is used rather than after — the mechanism is checked, then relied on.
	// The order also decides what a failure costs: everything below this line runs while HOLDING the
	// lock, and a panic down there leaves the record behind for the abandonment window to clear, so a
	// door that can fail is cheaper on this side of it. See `TreeLockParity` for which terms of the
	// shared format are gated and which deliberately are not.
	requireTreeLockParity()

	// ⭐ **AND WHETHER ANY OTHER COMMAND IS ALREADY WRITING THIS TREE.** Two suites share
	// `<specDir>/.spec-tmp`, so one deletes what the other is compiling — a measured FALSE RED — and a
	// build running alongside replaces the very binary this run is drawing verdicts from. Asked here,
	// after every question that costs nothing, so a refusal is instant and before a worker exists.
	//
	// ⚠ A HARNESS FIXTURE SUB-RUN TAKES NO LOCK: it is spawned BY a parent suite that holds this same
	// tree (see `requireHarnessRefusalsFire`), so acquiring here would refuse against its own parent.
	// The predicate is the one the self-test already uses to recognise itself.
	let lockExit = acquireSuiteTreeLock(specDir)
	if lockExit != 0 'busyTree'
		return lockExit
	end 'busyTree'

	let suiteExit = runSpecSuite(args, specDir: specDir, harnessExe: harnessExe, effectiveTarget: effectiveTarget)
	releaseTreeLock()
	return suiteExit
end 'runSpecTest'

// The tree lock for a suite run — taken for every run except a fixture sub-run, whose parent holds it.
function acquireSuiteTreeLock(specDir FilePath) returns ExitCode
	if isHarnessFixtureSubRun(specDir) 'parentHoldsIt'
		return 0
	end 'parentHoldsIt'

	return acquireTreeLock(specDir)
end 'acquireSuiteTreeLock'

// The suite itself, split out so `runSpecTest` has ONE exit through which the tree lock is released —
// see `compileAndReport` for why that shape rather than a release at each of five returns.
function runSpecSuite(args MaxonArgs, specDir FilePath, harnessExe FilePath, effectiveTarget Target) returns ExitCode
	let run = try runSpecPool(harnessExe, specDir: specDir, filter: args.filter, updateRequired: args.updateRequired, workerCount: args.workers, effectiveTarget: effectiveTarget, includeNetwork: args.includeNetwork) otherwise (e) 'runErr'
		reportCompileError(e)
		return 1
	end 'runErr'

	// A run that selected NOTHING is a failure, not a pass. A mistyped `--filter`
	// would otherwise report "0 passed, 0 failed" and exit 0 — a green suite that
	// tested nothing, which is the most expensive kind of lie a test runner can
	// tell. It is worse still under `--update-required`, where an empty selection
	// silently regenerates no goldens while looking like it did the work.
	if run.results.count() == 0 'selectedNothing'
		// ⚠ **ON THIS PATH THE CENSUS COMES BEFORE THE VERDICT LINE, AND ONLY ON THIS PATH**, because here
		// it is the only thing that can explain it. A default `--filter=http-client.get` selects nothing at
		// all: every case it named reaches the public internet. "no tests selected" on its own states the
		// consequence and hides the cause.
		reportNetworkExclusions(run.networkExcluded)

		printError("error: no tests selected{filterSuffix(args.filter)}\n")
		return 1
	end 'selectedNothing'

	let testsExit = reportResults(run.results)

	// ⭐ WHAT THIS RUN NEVER EVEN TRIED — the live-network cases, named and counted. It heads the "not
	// run" family below because these are the ones NOTHING about this machine prevented: a reader who is
	// not told sees a green column over a claim nobody exercised. It sits UNDER the verdicts rather than
	// above them for `reportTargetSkips`'s reason — a note printed ahead of a few thousand report lines
	// has scrolled away by the time the reader reaches the summary it qualifies.
	reportNetworkExclusions(run.networkExcluded)

	// ⭐ WHAT THIS RUN DID NOT RUN, AND WHY — immediately under the verdicts, because it is a statement
	// about the TESTS and must not sit below the two reports that are about the TREE. A skip is the one
	// outcome that can look like coverage without being any, so it is named, counted and attributed to
	// the target here rather than being left to the reader to notice a missing line.
	reportTargetSkips(run.results, effectiveTarget: effectiveTarget)

	// ⭐ AND WHAT THIS HOST COULD NOT EXECUTE, in the same slot and for the same reason — the other way
	// a case can be counted without having been verified. One sentence for the whole lane, because the
	// reason is the (target, host) pair rather than anything about a case.
	reportUnrunHere(tallyResults(run.results), effectiveTarget: effectiveTarget)

	// AFTER the summary and BEFORE the exit branches, so a red run reports its cost too: a suite that
	// went red because something got dramatically slower is exactly when the number is worth reading.
	// STDERR, because stdout is the report and must stay byte-identical between worker counts — see
	// `SuiteCpuTally.reportLine`, which is the only place this line is spelled.
	printError("{run.cpu.reportLine(run.poolSize)}\n")

	// ⚖ **WHAT THE GOLDENS HAVE TO SAY — REPORTED, NEVER JUDGED** (user, 2026-08-02: *"the goldens are
	// NOT supposed to be a gate, they are just for reference"*). Both reports come AFTER the verdicts
	// and the summary, on stderr, because they are facts about the TREE rather than about any test and
	// must never displace the reason a test failed. Neither returns an exit code: see `GoldenTracking`
	// for what a gate here cost us. Asked on every run, filtered included — a filtered run is exactly
	// where an agent mints a new spec's goldens and never sees that they are untracked.
	reportGoldenDrift(run.results)
	reportGoldenCensus(run.results, effectiveTarget: effectiveTarget)

	// ⭐ THE CENSUS'S MISSING FOURTH COLUMN, and it sits directly under it because it answers the
	// question the other three cannot even ask: a golden nothing COMPARES. All four columns above are
	// keyed on a case that produced a result, so a `.test` whose case was renamed away contributes to
	// none of them — see `GoldenTracking.reportUncomparedGoldens`.
	reportUncomparedGoldens(specDir, effectiveTarget: effectiveTarget)

	reportUntrackedGoldens(specDir)

	if testsExit != 0 'someTestFailed'
		return testsExit
	end 'someTestFailed'

	// Every test passed — and a worker that exited NON-ZERO is still a suite failure. A worker is
	// itself a leak-gated Maxon program, so exit 101 means the harness leaked memory while
	// servicing its tests. Green tests must never be allowed to mask that.
	if run.failedWorkers > 0 'workerFailed'
		printError("error: {run.failedWorkers} spec worker(s) exited non-zero (see above) — failing the suite despite {run.results.count()} passing tests\n")
		return 1
	end 'workerFailed'

	return 0
end 'runSpecSuite'

// The trailing ` matching --filter=<pattern>` clause of the "no tests selected"
// message, omitted entirely when no filter was given (an empty spec directory and
// a filter that matched nothing are different failures and read differently).
function filterSuffix(filter String) returns String
	if filter.isEmpty() 'noFilter'
		return ""
	end 'noFilter'
	return " matching --filter={filter}"
end 'filterSuffix'

// Resolve the spec directory: an explicit positional argument wins; otherwise
// the default `specs-shv2`.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE ANSWER IS ABSOLUTE WHICHEVER WAY IT WAS SPELLED, AND SINCE R4.8 THAT IS A CORRECTNESS
// REQUIREMENT RATHER THAN TIDINESS.** Every path this run computes hangs off this one — the staged
// source, the `-o` output base, the exe that gets SPAWNED (`SpecTestRunner.spawnOneRun`, or the batch
// script `runWslBatch` writes) — and that spawn
// now names its own working directory (`specRunWorkingDir`, `<checkoutRoot>/temp/`). A path relative to
// the HARNESS's cwd handed to a child that no longer has that cwd is a path with two possible meanings,
// and which one the OS picks is a PLATFORM detail: Windows resolves a partial `lpApplicationName`
// against the CALLING process's directory (so it happens to work), while the arm64/macOS runtime does
// `posix_spawn_file_actions_addchdir_np` and then `posix_spawnp`, which resolves it against the NEW one
// (so it does not). A path that survives the crossing is the only kind this harness may hand out.
//
// ⚠ The default branch was ALREADY absolute (`Directory.currentPath().join(…)`) and the explicit branch
// was not — one rule written twice, agreeing only because nothing in-tree passed a positional argument
// except `HarnessSelfTest`, which passes a RELATIVE one. Both branches now go through the same
// `resolve`, which is `join` for a relative spelling and the identity for an absolute one.
function resolveSpecDir(args MaxonArgs) returns FilePath throws FilePathError
	// Bind the FilePath to a local first: a bare String constant is ambiguous between the
	// String/FilePath overloads, and an inline `try` expression in an argument position does not
	// disambiguate it.
	let spelled = try FilePath.from(specDirSpelling(args))
	return spelled.resolve(Directory.currentPath())
end 'resolveSpecDir'

// The spec directory AS TYPED — the positional argument, or the default when there was none. Split out
// so the "make it absolute" rule above reads once instead of once per branch.
function specDirSpelling(args MaxonArgs) returns String
	let spelled = args.solePositional()
	if spelled.isEmpty() 'useDefault'
		return DefaultSpecDir
	end 'useDefault'
	return spelled
end 'specDirSpelling'

// ⭐ **THE COMMANDS THAT TAKE AT MOST ONE POSITIONAL REFUSE A SECOND, rather than obeying the first
// and dropping the rest — TRUE means it refused, and printed why.** `build` is the one command that
// reads the whole list (A3m); for every other, a second positional is a command line the driver only
// partly understood, and `main`'s `badOption` gate exists because proceeding on one of those is the
// failure a caller can neither see nor recover from. An unrecognized FLAG was already refused there;
// a second bare word was not, so `spec-test specs-shv2 specs` ran the first directory and said
// nothing at all about the second.
// ⚠ **`allowed` IS A COUNT AND NOT A BOOL, BECAUSE ONE COMMAND ALLOWS NONE.** `build`, `spec-test` and
// `verify-warm-rebuild` each read ONE positional and must refuse a second; `scale-test` reads NONE, so
// for it the FIRST is already the typo — measured, it ran the whole ladder for `scale-test foo` and
// never mentioned `foo`. A second copy of these two lines under a different predicate is how that
// message drifts, so the predicate is the parameter and the message stays here.
//
// Exported for `Testing/ScaleTestRunner`, which owns `scale-test`'s entry point the way this file owns
// the other three.
module function refusedPositionalBeyond(args MaxonArgs, allowed PositionalCount, usage String) returns bool
	if args.paths.count() <= allowed 'withinBudget'
		return false
	end 'withinBudget'

	let extra = try args.paths.get(allowed) otherwise panic("refusedPositionalBeyond: the count is greater than {allowed} on the line above, so index {allowed} exists")
	printError("error: unexpected extra argument: {extra}\n")
	printError("Usage: {usage}\n")
	return true
end 'refusedPositionalBeyond'

// How many positional arguments a command reads. Two today — one, or none.
typealias PositionalCount = int(0 to 1)

// ⭐⭐ **WHAT A WITHDRAWN OPTION SAYS — one sentence per option this driver once had.**
//
// It is its OWN message and not `badOption`'s because the two are different facts about the reader:
// an unknown option was never understood and is probably a typo, while a withdrawn one WAS
// understood and is now structurally impossible. Only the second owes an explanation, and a script
// carrying the flag needs to be edited rather than re-read.
//
// There is exactly ONE today, so this is a check and a sentence rather than a table. A second would
// make it a table; a second spelled inline would make it two answers to one question.
function withdrawnOptionMessage(option String) returns String
	if option.startsWith(WithdrawnCompilerFlagPrefix) 'theCompilerFlag'
		return "error: {WithdrawnCompilerFlagPrefix}= was REMOVED. spec-test compiles every selected test IN THIS PROCESS, so the compiler under test is this executable's own code and no path can name a different one. Run the suite with the binary you mean to test: `<compiler> {SpecTestCommandName}`."
	end 'theCompilerFlag'

	panic("withdrawnOptionMessage: '{option}' was recorded as a withdrawn option by `MaxonArgs.parse`, and this is the one place that says what replaced one — a flag recorded there and unnamed here would be refused with an empty sentence")
end 'withdrawnOptionMessage'

// Print each test's verdict and the summary, returning the process exit code (1
// iff any test failed). The PASS/FAIL line is built via a match EXPRESSION (both
// arms yield a real string) so the fail reason is extracted without a sentinel.
//
// ⚖ **THESE TWO NUMBERS ARE THE PRODUCT, AND NOTHING ABOUT A GOLDEN IS IN THEM** (user, 2026-08-02:
// *"the gate is the spec tests passing"*). A test whose committed golden drifted still PASSED — it is
// counted as one, printed as one, and the drift is reported separately and quietly on stderr
// (`reportGoldenDrift`). `0 failed` means the spec tests passed, with no qualifications to read past.
//
// ⚠ **A CASE THAT DID NOT RUN IS IN NEITHER NUMBER, AND ITS OWN LINE IS WHY THAT IS SAFE.** Counting
// it as a pass would be a claim nothing supports, and counting it as a failure would redden every
// cross-target run over a machine limit that is not a defect. The summary line's shape is deliberately
// untouched — several scripts read `^N passed, M failed` — so each count follows on a line of its own,
// and the reason behind each is named by its own note on stderr (`reportTargetSkips` names the
// constructs; `reportUnrunHere` names the host limit, once, because every case shares it).
function reportResults(results SpecTestResultArray) returns ExitCode
	for result in results 'each'
		print("{verdictLine(result.outcome, label: testLabel(result.specName, testName: result.testName))}\n")
	end 'each'

	let tally = tallyResults(results)
	print("\n{tally.passed} passed, {tally.failed} failed\n")

	// Silent at zero, which is the host lane and most of every other — the convention `reportGoldenDrift`
	// already sets, where "no note" means none.
	if tally.skipped > 0 'anySkipped'
		print("{tally.skipped} skipped — NOT RUN on this target (see the note on stderr for the constructs)\n")
	end 'anySkipped'

	if tally.notRunHere > 0 'anyUnrunnable'
		print("{tally.notRunHere} not run here — COMPILED and compared against their goldens, but this host cannot execute them (see the note on stderr)\n")
	end 'anyUnrunnable'

	// ⛔ **A RUN IN WHICH NOTHING RAN IS A FAILURE, AND IT IS THE SAME FAILURE `runSpecSuite`'s
	// empty-selection guard refuses one step earlier.** There the filter selected nothing; here
	// everything it selected was refused by the target or unexecutable on this host. Both print
	// `0 passed, 0 failed` and would otherwise exit 0 — a green run that tested nothing, which this
	// runner already calls the most expensive lie it can tell — and the only difference between them is
	// which side of the compile the emptiness arrived from. It is also the backstop on both not-run
	// mechanisms: a compiler that started emitting E3104 everywhere, or a lane on which literally every
	// case needs a runner this host lacks, would vanish the whole suite, and this is the line that
	// refuses to call that green.
	if tally.provedNothing() 'nothingRan'
		printError("error: every selected test was SKIPPED or NOT RUN on this host, so this run proved nothing — the notes below name the constructs this target has no implementation for and the binaries this host cannot execute\n")
		return 1
	end 'nothingRan'

	if tally.failed > 0 'anyFailed'
		return 1
	end 'anyFailed'
	return 0
end 'reportResults'

// One test's line in the report. A match EXPRESSION (every arm yields a real string) so the fail
// reason is extracted without a sentinel; split out of the loop above so the printed text and the
// tally beside it are not one `match` doing two jobs.
//
// A SKIP prints no reason: the reason is the compiler's E3104 diagnostic, which names an absolute
// scratch path and is genuinely multi-line, and it is printed once per case in the stderr census
// instead of being crammed onto a verdict line. A NOTRUN prints none either, for the opposite reason —
// every case on such a lane shares ONE reason, so it is stated once in the trailer instead of several
// thousand times here.
module function verdictLine(outcome TestOutcome, label String) returns String
	return match outcome 'verdict'
		pass gives "PASS {label}"
		fail(reason) gives "FAIL {label}: {reason}"
		skipped gives "SKIP {label}"
		notRunHere gives "NOTRUN {label}"
	end 'verdict'
end 'verdictLine'

// The FIRST bad option wins. A command line can be wrong in several ways at once, and reporting the
// last one found would point at whichever flag happened to be typed rightmost rather than at the one
// the reader would look for first.
function firstBadOption(current String, arg String) returns String
	if current.isEmpty() 'firstBad'
		return arg
	end 'firstBad'
	return current
end 'firstBadOption'

// ⭐⭐ **THE USER-FACING MESSAGE FOR A FILE/IO `CompileError`, WRITTEN THROUGH THE ONE DIAGNOSTIC
// SINK — so a caller compiling IN PROCESS reads exactly what a command line would have printed.**
//
// ⛔ **IT USED TO CALL `printError` DIRECTLY, AND THAT WAS THE ONE GAP IN THE CAPTURE.**
// `Diagnostics.emitDiagnosticLine`'s header recorded it as an outstanding obligation: every
// `Diagnostic` went through the sink, but the SIX `CompileError` arms that never reach
// `project.diagnostics` at all — `fileNotFound`, `directoryNotFound`, `noSourceFiles`, `readError`,
// `noCommonRoot`, `stdlibNotFound` — were printed here, past it. So a capture was exact for a REFUSED compile and
// EMPTY for a build that died on a missing file. `SpecTestRunner.compileOneTest` calls this INSIDE its
// capture, which is what closes that gap and why there is still only one renderer of these sentences.
//
// The `compileError` arm is SILENT: its diagnostics were already emitted by the pipeline, through this
// same sink, so a failing build says exactly those diagnostic lines and nothing else (the
// `maxoncstderr` spec contract).
//
// ⚠ THE TRAILING NEWLINE IS THE SINK'S NOW, NOT EACH ARM'S. `emitDiagnosticLine` terminates what it is
// handed (one call is one diagnostic), so an arm that kept its own would emit a blank line after every
// message.
//
// Shared by every command's failure path so the mapping lives in one place — including the scale-test
// runner, which is why it is exported.
module function reportCompileError(e CompileError)
	match e 'report'
		fileNotFound(p) then emitDiagnosticLine("error: file not found: {p}")
		directoryNotFound(p) then emitDiagnosticLine("error: directory not found: {p}")
		noSourceFiles(r) then emitDiagnosticLine("error: no source files under {r}")
		// NOT "share no directory": that is true of two drives and FALSE of two top-level directories on
		// one, whose common directory is the drive root — a directory `FilePath` cannot hold, because
		// `parent` cuts the separator off (see `Compiler.commonAncestorOf`). The sentence names what the
		// compiler can and cannot do rather than asserting something about the filesystem that is wrong
		// half the time, and the remedy works for both causes.
		noCommonRoot(first, other) then emitDiagnosticLine("error: {first} and {other} have no common directory this build can be rooted at. Name a directory they are both under as one of the paths, or build them separately")
		readError(p) then emitDiagnosticLine("error: could not read {p}")
		stdlibNotFound then emitDiagnosticLine("error: could not locate the stdlib/ directory (every compile loads stdlib source)")
		compileError then break 'report'
	end 'report'
end 'reportCompileError'

function unknownCommand() returns ExitCode
	printUsage()
	return 1
end 'unknownCommand'

function printUsage()
	print("Usage: maxon-shv2 <command> [options]\n")
	print("\n")
	print("Commands:\n")
	print("  build <file|directory>...     Compile a Maxon program to an executable. Several paths\n")
	print("                                are compiled as ONE program, in the order given\n")
	print("  verify-warm-rebuild <file>    Assert the query spine is deterministic + incremental\n")
	print("  verify-recheck <file|dir>     Assert ONE Project can be re-checked: two checks over\n")
	print("                                unchanged input agree, and a diagnostic introduced by an\n")
	print("                                edit CLEARS when the edit is undone. verify-warm-rebuild\n")
	print("                                gates the query spine without ever running the pipeline;\n")
	print("                                this gates the pipeline over it, which is what an editor\n")
	print("                                holding one project open for hours actually does.\n")
	print("  lsp-selftest                  Assert the LSP position conversion: the line index, and byte offset\n")
	print("                                <-> LSP Position where `character` counts UTF-16 CODE UNITS. Covers\n")
	print("                                2-, 3- and 4-byte characters, CRLF and the buffer edges. Compiler-\n")
	print("                                internal, so no specs-shv2 case can reach it.\n")
	print("  spec-test [dir]               Run the specs-shv2 suite (default dir: specs-shv2)\n")
	print("  scale-test                    Compile a ladder of generated programs, each rung double the\n")
	print("                                last, and report the per-phase MEMORY — allocations, frees and\n")
	print("                                bytes — plus the CPU TICKS the compiling thread spent. It does\n")
	print("                                NOT collect WALL time: wall time counts every other process on\n")
	print("                                the box, so a dated table of it would compare a loaded machine\n")
	print("                                in July against an idle one in August. The ladder doubles, so\n")
	print("                                the ratio between rungs IS the growth (x2.00 linear, x4.00\n")
	print("                                quadratic) and nothing is fitted. An INSTRUMENT, not a gate: it\n")
	print("                                renders no verdict, and it exits non-zero only when the RUN\n")
	print("                                broke. The trend it feeds is {OptimizationLogRelativePath}.\n")
	print("\n")
	print("Options:\n")
	print("  -o, --output=<path>           Output executable path (default: derived from input)\n")
	print("  --emit-ir                     Also write the lowered Target IR beside the exe as <output>.ir\n")
	print("  --emit-ir-runtime=<a>,<b>     Render these compiler-emitted functions in that IR too (implies --emit-ir)\n")
	print("  --target=<cpu>-<os>           (build, spec-test) Use this target instead of the host, e.g.\n")
	print("                                x64-windows, x64-linux, arm64-macos or wasm32-wasi (default: the host\n")
	print("                                target). For spec-test a cross target cross-compiles each\n")
	print("                                selected test and runs it under the vendored runtime.\n")
	print("  --metrics=<path>              (build) Write this compile's per-phase time + memory\n")
	print("                                attribution to <path> as TSV. `--log=compiler:debug` prints\n")
	print("                                the same numbers as a human-readable table.\n")
	print("  --filter=<pattern>            (spec-test) Run only tests whose `<spec>/<test>` label contains\n")
	print("                                <pattern>. A bare spec name selects that whole spec.\n")
	print("  --update-required             (spec-test) Rewrite the committed .test goldens instead of\n")
	print("                                checking against them. Running a test proves its allocation\n")
	print("                                is CORRECT; only the pinned IR proves the code did not get\n")
	print("                                WORSE. REVIEW THE RESULTING DIFF. Pair it with --filter: an\n")
	print("                                unfiltered run rewrites EVERY golden in the suite.\n")
	print("  --network                     (spec-test) Also run the cases marked `{NetworkMarker} {NetworkLiveValue} -->`,\n")
	print("                                which open a socket to a REAL EXTERNAL HOST (tcpbin.com,\n")
	print("                                httpbin.org). They are OUT of a default run because their\n")
	print("                                verdict measures somebody else's uptime rather than this\n")
	print("                                compiler; a default run NAMES every one it left out.\n")
	print("  --workers=<n>                 (spec-test) Run the suite on <n> persistent worker\n")
	print("                                subprocesses (default {defaultWorkerCount()}, this machine). 1 is this same pool with one\n")
	print("                                worker in it, NOT a serial path — every worker count must\n")
	print("                                print BYTE-IDENTICAL output, and that invariance is the\n")
	print("                                pool's sharpest gate.\n")
	print("  --rungs=<n>                   (scale-test) Climb <n> rungs (default {DefaultRungCount},\n")
	print("                                range {MinRungCount}-{MaxRungCount}). Each rung DOUBLES the\n")
	print("                                program, so this is exponential in cost.\n")
	print("  --repeat=<n>                  (scale-test) Compile every rung <n> times (default\n")
	print("                                {DefaultRepeatCount}, range {MinRepeatCount}-{MaxRepeatCount}) and report the LEAST CPU ticks\n")
	print("                                any of them spent in each phase — the least-disturbed sample,\n")
	print("                                since noise can only ADD to a CPU reading and a mean would be\n")
	print("                                dragged by one stall. The MEMORY columns take nothing from a\n")
	print("                                repeat: they are exact, so they come from the first compile and\n")
	print("                                are CROSS-CHECKED for equality against the rest. A memory number\n")
	print("                                that differs between two compiles of one rung is a BROKEN RUN.\n")
	print("  --note=<text>                 (scale-test) Record this run in {OptimizationLogRelativePath}\n")
	print("                                — one dated row of the per-rung allocations and bytes, with\n")
	print("                                <text> as the reason. Without it a run reports and records\n")
	print("                                nothing; WITH it the row is written, moved or not, because it is\n")
	print("                                an INSTRUCTION. The instrument can see exactly WHAT moved and can\n")
	print("                                never see why; you are the only one who knows, and only now.\n")
	print("  --per-type                    (scale-test) Also run the --mm-trace pass, attributing\n")
	print("                                allocations to the TYPE allocated AND to the SCOPE that\n")
	print("                                allocated it — the scope table is the one that finds things. Off\n")
	print("                                by default: it is slow, and it is a different binary from the\n")
	print("                                one being measured.\n")
	print("  --emit-corpus=<dir>           (scale-test) Dump the generated corpus to <dir> for inspection\n")
	print("                                (it is otherwise built into a temp dir and thrown away).\n")
	print("  --result-json=<path>          (scale-test) Write the whole run — per-rung and per-phase memory,\n")
	print("                                and the deltas from the last recorded run — to <path> as JSON, so\n")
	print("                                a tool never has to scrape the human report.\n")
end 'printUsage'

// The parsed command the driver dispatches on.
enum MaxonCommand
	build
	verifyWarmRebuild
	verifyRecheck
	lspSelfTest
	specTest
	scaleTest
	unknown
end 'MaxonCommand'

// The positional arguments AS TYPED — source path SPELLINGS, not yet `FilePath`s, because a spelling
// that is not a valid path is a command-line error the driver reports with the text the caller wrote.
// A distinctly-named alias rather than `Project.StringArray` for the reason `MaxonSourcePathArray`
// gives next door: a name that says what the list is FOR cannot be confused with the several other
// `Array with String` aliases this compiler already carries.
export typealias SourcePathSpellingArray = Array with String

// Thin M1 argument model: a command, its input paths, an optional output override,
// and the `--emit-ir` toggle. Grows into v1's full option set (targets, logging,
// cache flags, ...) as the driver gains those capabilities.
// Exported: the scale-test runner lives in Testing/ and takes the parsed args, exactly as the
// spec-test runner takes them from inside Main.
module type MaxonArgs
	export var command as MaxonCommand
	// ⭐⭐ **EVERY POSITIONAL, IN THE ORDER TYPED (A3m).** `build` compiles them all and the ORDER is
	// load-bearing — `Compiler.loadSourceFiles` registers sources in exactly this order, which is how
	// a caller states which of two sibling files declares first without the loader ever sorting (see
	// that function, and `StdlibLoader`'s no-sort ruling). Every other command takes at most one and
	// `refusedPositionalBeyond` says so; before A3m the parser kept the first and dropped the rest
	// here, silently, without even setting `badOption`.
	export var paths as SourcePathSpellingArray
	export var outputOverride as String
	export var emitIr as bool
	// Whether this build emits the green-thread async trace (`--async-trace`). See `AsyncTraceFlag`.
	export var asyncTrace as bool
	// Whether this build emits the DebugStream producer (`--debugstream`). See `DebugStreamFlag`.
	export var debugStream as bool
	// `--emit-ir-runtime=<name>,<name>` (build only): the compiler-EMITTED functions the IR sidecar
	// must ALSO render. EMPTY is the ordinary build and is the literal count of names given, not a
	// sentinel. It IMPLIES `--emit-ir` (see `parse`), because a list of functions to print is a
	// request for the printing.
	export var runtimeIrNames as RuntimeFunctionNameArray
	// `--filter=<pattern>` (spec-test only): run only the tests whose `<spec>/<test>` label
	// contains <pattern>. Empty selects the whole suite.
	export var filter as String
	// `--update-required` (spec-test only): REWRITE the committed `.test` goldens instead of
	// checking the emitted IR against them. The goldens are a code-quality gate — running a test
	// proves its allocation is CORRECT, but only the pinned IR can say the code did not get WORSE
	// (an extra spill still computes the right answer). So regenerating them is the one way a
	// codegen change enters the tree, and it is deliberately an explicit act: the diff is the review.
	export var updateRequired as bool
	// `--workers=<n>` (spec-test only): how many persistent worker subprocesses the pool runs. 1 is
	// the SAME pool with one worker in it, not a serial in-process branch — see runSpecTest.
	export var workers as WorkerCount
	// `--worker-persistent` (spec-test only, INTERNAL): this process IS a pool worker. Set by the
	// parent dispatcher on the command line it spawns, never typed by a person, and therefore left
	// out of the usage text. Undocumented but PARSED: an unrecognized flag aborts the run, so a
	// worker whose own flag was not understood would fail to start at all.
	export var workerPersistent as bool
	// `--metrics=<path>` (build only): write this compile's per-phase time + memory attribution as
	// TSV to <path> (see MetricsEmit). The scale-test harness reads it back; a human wants
	// `--log=compiler:debug`, which prints the same numbers as a table.
	export var metricsPath as String
	// `--emit-corpus=<dir>` (scale-test only): dump the generated scaling corpus to <dir> for
	// inspection instead of the temp directory it is otherwise built into and thrown away.
	export var emitCorpus as String
	// `--rungs=<n>` (scale-test only): how many rungs of the ladder to climb. Each doubles the
	// program, so this is exponential in cost — see ScaleCorpus.
	export var rungs as RungCount
	// `--repeat=<n>` (scale-test only): how many times each rung is compiled. The CPU column takes
	// the MINIMUM of the repeats (the least-disturbed sample); the memory columns take the first and
	// are cross-checked for equality across the rest, since they are bit-for-bit reproducible and a
	// difference is a broken run — see ScaleTestRunner's header.
	export var repeat as RepeatCount
	// `--per-type` (scale-test only): also run the untimed `--mm-trace` pass, which attributes
	// allocations to the TYPE allocated AND to the SCOPE that allocated it. Off by default: it traces
	// every allocation individually and is far slower than the timed run.
	export var perType as bool
	// `--note=<text>` (scale-test only): RECORD this run in docs/optimization-log.md, with <text> as
	// the reason. A run without it reports and records nothing — scale-test is run dozens of times a
	// day against dirty trees, and a log of all of them would be a log nobody reads. WITH it the row is
	// written, whether or not a number moved: it is an INSTRUCTION from the operator, who is the only
	// party who knows whether the run is a datapoint. The instrument can see exactly WHAT moved and can
	// never see why, so the reason is demanded at the one moment it is still known — see
	// Testing/ScaleHistory.maxon.
	export var note as String
	// `--result-json=<path>` (scale-test only): write the run's full result — per-rung and per-phase
	// memory, and the deltas from the last recorded run — as JSON.
	//
	// This exists so the MCP tool does not SCRAPE the human report. The report's columns are padded
	// to be read by a person; the day a phase name outgrows its column, a scrape would silently
	// mis-parse rather than fail. A caller that wants data gets data.
	export var resultJson as String
	// `--target=<cpu>-<os>` (build, spec-test): use this target instead of the host
	// (`detectHostTarget`). Empty selects the host. Resolved to a Target via `resolveTargetFlag` in
	// both `runBuild` and `runSpecTest`; an unknown spelling aborts. Needed to cross-compile
	// x64-windows on an arm64 host for golden verification, to select arm64-macos explicitly, and to
	// drive the spec suite through the wasm32-wasi backend (cross-compile-and-run under wasmtime).
	export var targetSpec as String
	// Whether this spec run may reach a real external host (`--network`). FALSE by default, and the
	// default is the whole point — see `NetworkFlag`.
	export var includeNetwork as bool
	// Set when an argument could not be understood. The driver refuses to run rather
	// than proceed on a command line it only partly parsed — see `parse`.
	export var badOption as String
	// ⭐ Set when an argument names an option this driver ONCE HAD and deliberately withdrew. A
	// separate field from `badOption` because the two owe the reader different sentences and `main`
	// prints them in different words: an unknown option was never understood, a withdrawn one was and
	// is now structurally impossible. See `withdrawnOptionMessage`.
	export var withdrawnOption as String

	// ⭐ **THE COMMAND LINE WITH NOTHING ON IT** — every field at the value an absent flag leaves it.
	// The defaults live here, in ONE place, rather than in `parse`'s locals: `parse` accumulates into a
	// record precisely so that it HAS no per-flag local (see its own note), and a default written at the
	// declaration is a default no arm can forget to carry through.
	//
	// ⚠ **EVERY FIELD ABOVE IS `var` FOR THAT ONE CALLER.** Nothing else mutates a `MaxonArgs`, and
	// nothing can through the `let` binding `main` holds it by (E3063) — the tier states who may write,
	// and the binding states whether anyone does.
	export static function create() returns MaxonArgs
		return Self{command: MaxonCommand.unknown, paths: SourcePathSpellingArray.create(), outputOverride: "", emitIr: false, asyncTrace: false, debugStream: false, runtimeIrNames: RuntimeFunctionNameArray.create(), filter: "", updateRequired: false, workers: defaultWorkerCount(), workerPersistent: false, metricsPath: "", emitCorpus: "", rungs: DefaultRungCount, repeat: DefaultRepeatCount, perType: false, note: "", resultJson: "", targetSpec: "", includeNetwork: false, badOption: "", withdrawnOption: ""}
	end 'create'

	// The one positional a command that takes at most one was given, or "" when there was none —
	// the same "empty means absent" convention every other optional field here uses
	// (`outputOverride`, `filter`, `note`). A SECOND positional never reaches this: the
	// commands that read it call `refusedPositionalBeyond` first, so empty cannot mean two things.
	export function solePositional() returns String
		return try self.paths.get(0) otherwise ""
	end 'solePositional'

	// Parse the process command line. argv[0] is the executable path (skipped).
	// The first `build` token selects the command; every bare (non-flag)
	// argument is KEPT, in order, as a positional; `-o <path>` / `--output=<path>` set the output;
	// `--emit-ir` requests the Target-IR sidecar; `--filter=<pattern>` narrows the
	// spec-test selection; `--update-required` regenerates the spec-test goldens.
	//
	// An unrecognized `-`-leading argument is RECORDED, not ignored. It used to fall
	// through to the positional branch and become the input path, so `spec-test
	// --mm-trace` quietly looked for a spec directory literally named `--mm-trace`
	// and reported an empty suite. A flag the driver does not implement must fail
	// loudly, not be absorbed into an unrelated slot.
	export static function parse() returns MaxonArgs
		// ⚠ **THE WORKING SET IS ONE RECORD BECAUSE TWENTY-ONE `var`s DO NOT FIT IN x64's REGISTERS**
		// (`E5001`). A `var` assigned in one arm of this chain is handed on at every join, so each one
		// becomes a phi on the loop header and the loop carries a register per flag — MEASURED at 33
		// values live against 14 GPRs, which is a refusal, not a slowdown. `parsed.filter = …` is a FIELD
		// STORE into one frame slot, so the loop carries ONE value however many flags are added below.
		// ⇒ Adding a flag means adding a FIELD, never a local.
		//
		// ⚠ **AND A GREEN SUITE ON ANOTHER HOST WOULD NOT HAVE CAUGHT IT.** The refusal is x64's alone:
		// arm64 allocates from 26 GPRs and fits a shape x64's 14 cannot, which is why
		// `specs-shv2/register-pressure.md`'s `assigned-only-loop-carried-vars-overflow` — the smaller
		// twin of this loop — carries `targets: x64-windows, x64-linux`.
		var parsed = Self.create()

		let args = CommandLine.args()
		var i = 1
		while i < args.count() 'each'
			let arg = try args.get(i) otherwise panic("MaxonArgs.parse: index {i} >= args.count() despite the while guard i<args.count()")
			if arg == "build" 'buildCmd'
				parsed.command = MaxonCommand.build
			end 'buildCmd' else if arg == "verify-warm-rebuild" 'verifyCmd'
				parsed.command = MaxonCommand.verifyWarmRebuild
			end 'verifyCmd' else if arg == "verify-recheck" 'recheckCmd'
				parsed.command = MaxonCommand.verifyRecheck
			end 'recheckCmd' else if arg == "lsp-selftest" 'lspSelfTestCmd'
				parsed.command = MaxonCommand.lspSelfTest
			end 'lspSelfTestCmd' else if arg == SpecTestCommandName 'specTestCmd'
				parsed.command = MaxonCommand.specTest
			end 'specTestCmd' else if arg == "scale-test" 'scaleTestCmd'
				parsed.command = MaxonCommand.scaleTest
			end 'scaleTestCmd' else if arg == "-o" 'dashOutput'
				i = i + 1
				parsed.outputOverride = try args.get(i) otherwise ""
			end 'dashOutput' else if arg.startsWith("--output=") 'longOutput'
				parsed.outputOverride = try CommandLine.optionValue(arg) otherwise ""
			end 'longOutput' else if arg == EmitIrFlag 'emitIrFlag'
				parsed.emitIr = true
			end 'emitIrFlag' else if arg == AsyncTraceFlag 'asyncTraceFlag'
				parsed.asyncTrace = true
			end 'asyncTraceFlag' else if arg == DebugStreamFlag 'debugStreamFlag'
				parsed.debugStream = true
			end 'debugStreamFlag' else if arg.startsWith(EmitIrRuntimeFlagPrefix) 'emitIrRuntimeFlag'
				// ⚠ It cannot be swallowed by the arm above even though it shares its prefix: that one
				// tests `==`, and this one is the only arm that tests this prefix.
				// ⚠ **THE CHECK IS ON THE PARSED NAMES, NOT ON THE RAW VALUE, and that is the whole
				// point of doing it after the split.** A valueless `--emit-ir-runtime=` is a BAD OPTION,
				// not an empty list — the same door `--compiler=` refuses through — but so is
				// `--emit-ir-runtime=,` and `--emit-ir-runtime=" "`, which carry a NON-empty value that
				// names no function: `splitRuntimeNames` drops empty parts, so a raw-value check passed
				// them straight through to an empty list. Empty is how this field spells "the caller
				// named no runtime function", so either spelling would render a sidecar with no runtime
				// body under a command line that asked for one, and the golden built from it would pin
				// exactly what it pinned before while claiming otherwise. One check, on the value that
				// actually reaches the printer.
				let named = splitRuntimeNames(try CommandLine.optionValue(arg) otherwise "")
				if named.count() == 0 'noRuntimeNames'
					parsed.badOption = firstBadOption(parsed.badOption, arg: arg)
				end 'noRuntimeNames' else 'hasRuntimeNames'
					parsed.runtimeIrNames = named
					// A list of functions to PRINT is a request for the printing. Without this the flag
					// would be silently inert whenever `--emit-ir` was forgotten — a no-op that looks like
					// a rendering, which is the one outcome the printer's own refusals exist to prevent.
					parsed.emitIr = true
				end 'hasRuntimeNames'
			end 'emitIrRuntimeFlag' else if arg.startsWith(FilterFlagPrefix) 'filterFlag'
				parsed.filter = try CommandLine.optionValue(arg) otherwise ""
			end 'filterFlag' else if arg == UpdateRequiredFlag 'updateRequiredFlag'
				parsed.updateRequired = true
			end 'updateRequiredFlag' else if arg == NetworkFlag 'networkFlag'
				parsed.includeNetwork = true
			end 'networkFlag' else if arg.startsWith(WorkersFlagPrefix) 'workersFlag'
				let raw = try CommandLine.optionValue(arg) otherwise ""
				// A bad value is a BAD OPTION, not a clamp — the same door as `--rungs`. `--workers=0`
				// asks for a pool that can run nothing; quietly running the default instead would
				// answer a question the caller never asked. See SpecWorkerPool.parseWorkerCount.
				match parseWorkerCount(raw) 'workersArg'
					valid(count) then parsed.workers = count
					invalid then parsed.badOption = firstBadOption(parsed.badOption, arg: arg)
				end 'workersArg'
			end 'workersFlag' else if arg == WorkerPersistentFlag 'workerPersistentFlag'
				parsed.workerPersistent = true
			end 'workerPersistentFlag' else if arg.startsWith(WithdrawnCompilerFlagPrefix) 'withdrawnCompilerFlag'
				// ⛔ RECORDED AS WITHDRAWN, NOT AS UNKNOWN — see `WithdrawnCompilerFlagPrefix` and
				// `withdrawnOptionMessage`. The prefix is matched WITHOUT its `=`, so `--compiler`,
				// `--compiler=`, and `--compiler=<path>` all land here: every spelling a caller might still
				// be passing gets the sentence that says what happened to it, rather than only the one that
				// happens to be well-formed.
				//
				// ⚠ IT MUST SIT AHEAD OF THE ANONYMOUS `-`-leading arm below, which would otherwise swallow
				// it into `badOption` — the exact fall-through this refusal exists to avoid.
				parsed.withdrawnOption = firstBadOption(parsed.withdrawnOption, arg: arg)
			end 'withdrawnCompilerFlag' else if arg.startsWith(MetricsFlagPrefix) 'metricsFlag'
				parsed.metricsPath = try CommandLine.optionValue(arg) otherwise ""
			end 'metricsFlag' else if arg.startsWith("--emit-corpus=") 'emitCorpusFlag'
				parsed.emitCorpus = try CommandLine.optionValue(arg) otherwise ""
			end 'emitCorpusFlag' else if arg.startsWith("--rungs=") 'rungsFlag'
				let raw = try CommandLine.optionValue(arg) otherwise ""
				// A bad value is recorded as a BAD OPTION, not clamped to the default. `main` refuses
				// to run on a command line it only partly understood, which is the only honest
				// response to `--rungs=99`: the caller asked for a ladder this suite will not climb
				// (each rung DOUBLES the program), and quietly climbing a different one would answer
				// a question they never asked. See ScaleCorpus.parseRungCount.
				match parseRungCount(raw) 'rungsArg'
					valid(count) then parsed.rungs = count
					invalid then parsed.badOption = firstBadOption(parsed.badOption, arg: arg)
				end 'rungsArg'
			end 'rungsFlag' else if arg.startsWith("--repeat=") 'repeatFlag'
				let raw = try CommandLine.optionValue(arg) otherwise ""
				// The same door as `--rungs` and `--workers`: a bad value is a BAD OPTION, not a
				// clamp. `--repeat=0` asks for a rung that is never compiled, and quietly compiling
				// it three times would answer a question the caller never asked — the run they get
				// back would then differ from the one they typed in the column that is least able to
				// prove it. See ScaleTestRunner.parseRepeatCount.
				match parseRepeatCount(raw) 'repeatArg'
					valid(count) then parsed.repeat = count
					invalid then parsed.badOption = firstBadOption(parsed.badOption, arg: arg)
				end 'repeatArg'
			end 'repeatFlag' else if arg == "--per-type" 'perTypeFlag'
				parsed.perType = true
			end 'perTypeFlag' else if arg.startsWith("--note=") 'noteFlag'
				parsed.note = try CommandLine.optionValue(arg) otherwise ""
			end 'noteFlag' else if arg.startsWith("--result-json=") 'resultJsonFlag'
				parsed.resultJson = try CommandLine.optionValue(arg) otherwise ""
			end 'resultJsonFlag' else if arg.startsWith(TargetFlagPrefix) 'targetFlag'
				parsed.targetSpec = try CommandLine.optionValue(arg) otherwise ""
			end 'targetFlag' else if arg.startsWith("--log=") 'logFlag'
				let spec = try CommandLine.optionValue(arg) otherwise ""
				if not parseLogOption(spec) 'badSpec'
					printError("Unrecognized --log spec '{spec}' (expected <level> or <category>:<level>)\n")
				end 'badSpec'
			end 'logFlag' else if arg.startsWith("-") 'unknownFlag'
				parsed.badOption = firstBadOption(parsed.badOption, arg: arg)
			end 'unknownFlag' else 'positional'
				// KEPT, never dropped. WHICH of them a command uses is the COMMAND's question — `build`
				// compiles them all in this order, everything else refuses a second — and that answer
				// cannot be given here, where the arity of the command being parsed is not yet known.
				parsed.paths.push(arg)
			end 'positional'
			i = i + 1
		end 'each'

		return parsed
	end 'parse'
end 'MaxonArgs'

// The function names an `--emit-ir-runtime=` value lists — split on the shared separator, each part
// trimmed, empties dropped. The same shape `parseTargetKeys` gives a `<!-- targets: … -->` value,
// because they are the same kind of list and a second spelling of "comma-separated" would be one
// more thing a reader has to check.
function splitRuntimeNames(value String) returns RuntimeFunctionNameArray
	var names = RuntimeFunctionNameArray.create()
	for part in value.split(RuntimeNameSeparator) 'eachName'
		let trimmed = part.trim()
		if not trimmed.isEmpty() 'nonEmpty'
			names.push(trimmed)
		end 'nonEmpty'
	end 'eachName'
	return names
end 'splitRuntimeNames'
